Let us fix $j<q_{R_A}$ which defines a highest and lowest weight $\mu_{top},\mu_{bot}\in  \Delta_{R_A}$. First, consider the highest weight component. In general, this belongs to a spin-$s_I$ representation of $SU(2)_I$. The fermions $\psi^a_{R_{A,s_I}}$ for $\mu_a \in \Delta^{(j)}_{R_{A,s_I}}$ are of the form 
\begin{align}\begin{split}\label{lemmatransform}
\psi_{R_{A,s_I}}^a=\sum_b {\cal D}^{(s_I)}_{s_I-a,s_I-b}(\theta,\phi)\left(\begin{array}{c}
f_{b}(r)\,e^{i \frac{\phi}{2}}{\cal D}^{(n)}_{-m-s_I+b,Q_{s_I}+\frac{1}{2}}(\theta,\phi)\\
g_b(r)\,e^{-i\frac{\phi}{2}}{\cal D}^{(n)}_{-m-s_I+b,Q_{s_I}-\frac{1}{2}}(\theta,\phi)
\end{array}\right)~. 
\end{split}\end{align}
Since the $\widetilde{\cal D}_\infty$ eigenvalues of such a solution are $\lambda_\pm^{(n)}=\pm \sqrt{(n+1/2)^2-Q_{s_I}^2}$, it follows that there exists a  spin down zero-eigenvector of $\widetilde{\cal D}_\infty$ with $n=Q_{s_I}-\frac{1}{2}$ iff $Q_{s_I}-\frac{1}{2}\geq0$ and it is the unique zero-eigenvector belonging to this ${\vec{I}}$-representation. Conversely, if $Q_{s_I}\leq 0$, then there is no spin down zero-eigenvector from the irreducible ${\vec{I}}$ representation. 

Thus, let us assume that $Q_{s_I}>0$. This condition combined with $q_{R_A}>j,s_I$ implies that 
\begin{align}\begin{split}\label{jrange}
Q_{s_I}-\frac{1}{2}+s_I\geq j\geq Q_{s_I}-\frac{1}{2}-s_I\geq0~.
\end{split}\end{align}
Now, let us pick $n=Q_{s_I}-\frac{1}{2}\geq0$. We now must show that the solutions constructed above have a non-trivial total spin-$j$ solution which requires showing that $[j]\in [n]\otimes [s_I]$. 

By using the standard multiplication of Wigner $D$-functions, we can expand the product of Wigner $D$-functions in \eqref{lemmatransform} as representations with total spin:
\begin{align}\begin{split}
\left[Q_{s_I}-\frac{1}{2}\right]\otimes \left[s_I\right]=\left[Q_{s_I}-\frac{1}{2}-s_I\right]\oplus \left[Q_{s_I}+\frac{1}{2}-s_I\right]\oplus...\oplus\left[Q_{s_I}-\frac{1}{2}+s_I\right]~,
\end{split}\end{align}
where $[p]$ is the representation of total spin-$p$. It then follows from \eqref{jrange} that $[j]\in \left[Q_{s_I}-1/2\right]\otimes \left[s_I\right]$ and that there is a single spin down zero-eigenvector of $\widetilde{\cal D}_\infty$ in the irreducible ${\vec{I}}$ component containing $\mu_{top}$ when $\langle \mu_{top},\gamma_m\rangle>0$.                                                                                                                           
The explicit solution is given by 
\begin{align}\begin{split}
f_a=0\quad, \quad g_a=\begin{cases}
 \left\langle j,Q_{s_I}+s_I-a-\frac{1}{2}\big{|}Q_{s_I}-\frac{1}{2},Q_{s_I}-\frac{1}{2};s_I,s_I-a\right\rangle&\mu_a\in s_I\\
0&else
\end{cases}
\end{split}\end{align}
where we have chosen a basis $\{\mu_a\}_{a=0}^{2s_I}$ of $\Delta_{R_{A,s_I}}$

Now, since the eigenvectors of $\widetilde{\cal D}_\infty$ decompose with respect to irreducible ${\vec{I}}$-representations, it follows that the unique spin down zero-eigenvector of $\psi_{R_A}$ with  total spin-$j$ of $\widetilde{\cal D}_\infty$ is the one constructed above containing $\mu_{top}\in  \Delta_{R_A}$. 
The reason is that any spin-$j$ eigenvector $F_0^{(j)}$ of $\widetilde{\cal D}_\infty$ that belongs to a different ${\vec{I}}$-irreducible component of spin-$s_I'$ (i.e. the spin-$s_I'$ representation does not contain $v_{\mu_{top}}$) will not be spin polarized as we have shown in Section \ref{sec:fermrep} by explicit construction. This follows from the fact that $[\gamma_m,{\vec{I}}]=0$ which, when restricted to an irreducible $SU(2)_T$ representation implies $Q_{s_I}\neq Q_{s_I'}$ even when $s_I=s_I'$. 

Then, since the gauge transformation $\omega$ that maps the abelian, asymptotic gauge to the canonical gauge commutes with $\vec{S}$, any such $F_0^{(j)}$ will necessarily correspond to a solution that is not polarized and hence with quantum number $n$ in the asymptotic gauge such that 
\begin{align}\begin{split}
n\neq \begin{cases}
Q_{s_I'}-\frac{1}{2} & Q_{s_I'}>0\\
-Q_{s_I'}+\frac{1}{2}&Q_{s_I'}<0
\end{cases}
\end{split}\end{align}
Using the fact that $\lambda^{(n)}_\pm=\pm \sqrt{(n+1/2)^2-Q_{s_I'}^2}$,  we see that any such $F_0^{(j)}$ cannot be a spin-$j$ zero-eigenvector of ${\cal D}_\infty$ and that the only spin-$j$ zero-energy solution is the one constructed above. 

Therefore, we see that there is only a single spin down zero-eigenvector of $\widetilde{\cal D}_\infty$ with total spin-$j$ iff  $\langle \mu_{top},T_3-I_3\rangle>0$ coming from the irreducible ${\vec{I}}$ component containing $\mu_{top}$ as constructed above. 
















A nearly identical argument shows that there only exists a spin up zero-eigenvector of $\widetilde{\cal D}_\infty$ when $\langle \mu_{bot},T_3-I_3\rangle<0$. \qed

From Lemma 2, we know that if  $\langle \mu_{top},T_3-I_3\rangle>0$ and $\langle \mu_{bot},T_3-I_3\rangle<0$ then there are a pair of zero-eigenvalues of $\widetilde{\cal D}_\infty$ that are oppositely spin polarized.                                                                                                           


Conversely, we see that if $\langle \mu_{top},T_3-I_3\rangle,\langle\mu_{bot},T_3-I_3\rangle\geq0$ or $\langle \mu_{top},T_3-I_3\rangle,\langle\mu_{bot},T_3-I_3\rangle\leq0$ there is at most a single spin polarized eigenvalue.                                                                              


Now, using the fact that $q_{R_A}>s_I$ and $[{\vec{I}},\gamma_m]=0$ for irreducible representations $R_A$, we see that if $\langle \mu_{top},T_3-I_3\rangle<0$ then $\langle \mu_{bot},T_3-I_3\rangle<0$ and similarly if $\langle \mu_{bot},T_3-I_3\rangle>0$ then $\langle \mu_{top},T_3\rangle>0$. This implies that if 
\begin{align}\begin{split}
\langle \mu_{top},T_3-I_3\rangle\langle\mu_{bot},T_3-I_3\rangle<0~,
\end{split}\end{align}
then $\langle \mu_{top},T_3-I_3\rangle>0$ and $\langle \mu_{bot},T_3-I_3\rangle<0$ and $\widetilde{\cal D}_\infty$ has two spin-$j$ zero-eigenvectors. 

Further, since we are considering $R_A$ as a representation of $SU(2)_T$, the unique non-trivial element $w$ of the Weyl group of $\mathfrak{su}(2)_T$ exchanges the top and bottom weight so that 
\begin{align}\begin{split}
\mu_{bot}=w(\mu_{top})~. 
\end{split}\end{align}
Explicitly, in terms of Dynkin indices for weights:
\begin{align}\begin{split}
\mu=[m_1,...,m_{N-1}]~~\Rightarrow ~~w(\mu)=[-m_{N-1},...,-m_1]~. \end{split}\end{align}
Therefore, there exists two zero-eigenvectors of $\widetilde{\cal D}_\infty$ if 
\begin{align}\begin{split}
\langle \mu_{top},T_3-I_3\rangle\langle\mu_{bot},T_3-I_3\rangle=\langle \mu_{top},T_3-I_3\rangle\langle w(\mu_{top}),T_3-I_3\rangle<0~.\end{split}\end{align}

\qed

First let us recall that a zero-eigenvalue of $\widetilde{\cal D}_\infty$ corresponds to a solution of the Dirac equation with asymptotic behavior 
\begin{align}\begin{split}
\lim_{r\to \infty}\psi_{R_A}\sim \frac{1}{r}\psi_{R_A}^{(0)}~. 
\end{split}\end{align}
And therefore, if there are no zero-eigenvalues of $\widetilde{\cal D}_\infty$, then 
\begin{align}\begin{split}
\lim_{r\to \infty}r\psi_{R_A}=0~.
\end{split}\end{align}

Now using the fact that for any non-trivial solution of the Dirac equation $\psi_{R_A}$:
\begin{align}\begin{split}
\int \partial_\mu \widetilde{J}_{R_A}^{~\,\mu}\,d^3x=\int |D_\mu \psi_{R_A}|^2\,d^3 x>0\quad, \quad \widetilde{J}^{~\,\mu}_{R_A}=\bar\psi_{R_A}\bar\sigma^a \Phi\psi_{R_A}~,
\end{split}\end{align} we see that 
\begin{align}\begin{split}
\lim_{r\to \infty}\int_{S^2} \widetilde{J}^r_{R_A}\, \,r^2\,d^2\Omega\neq0~~\Rightarrow~~\lim_{r\to \infty}r \,\psi_{R_A}(r)\neq 0~. 
\end{split}\end{align}
Thus, any solution of the Dirac equation $\psi_{R_A}$ must have leading order $1/r$ as $r\to \infty$. Therefore, if $\widetilde{\cal D}_\infty$ has no zero-eigenvalues, then all asymptotic solutions that fall off faster than $1/r$ and there can be no solution to the Dirac equation.
\qed

From Lemma 4, we see that any non-trivial solutions to the Dirac equation has 
\begin{align}\begin{split}
\lim_{r\to \infty}r\psi_R\neq 0~. 
\end{split}\end{align}
From our explicit solutions to the Dirac equation, we see that these solutions correspond to the zero-eigenvectors of $\widetilde{\cal D}_\infty$. From Lemma 2, we know that there are at most two such eigenvectors and that they are polarized. 

Then, for any time-independent solution of the Dirac equation $\psi_{R_A}$:
\begin{align}\begin{split}
\partial_\mu  J^{~\,\mu}_{R_A}=0\quad, \quad J_{R_A}^{~\,\mu}=\bar\psi_{R_A}\bar\sigma^\mu\psi_{R_A}~.
\end{split}\end{align}
This implies 
\begin{align}\begin{split}\label{polcond}
0=\int \partial_\mu  J^{~\,\mu}_{R_A}\,d^3x=\lim_{r\to \infty}\int J^r_{R_A}\,r^2\,d^2\Omega=\lim_{r\to \infty}r^2\sum_a\left(|f_a|^2-|g_a|^2\right)~.
\end{split}\end{align}
This constraint then implies that the two polarized zero-eigenvectors of $\widetilde{\cal D}_\infty$ can at most combine into one non-trivial solution of the Dirac equation. 

Further, if there is only a single zero-eigenvalue of $\widetilde{\cal D}_\infty$, then from Lemma 2 it must be spin polarized. The condition \eqref{polcond} then cannot be satisfied except by the trivial solution. Additionally, if there are no zero-eigenvalues of $\widetilde{\cal D}_\infty$, then there are no solutions that go like $1/r$ at $r\to \infty$ and hence from Lemma 4, we see that there can be no zero energy plane-wave normalizable solutions of the Dirac equation. 
\qed

From the above analysis, we see that the kernel of the Dirac operator when restricted to the $R_A$-irreducible representation of $SU(2)_T$ decomposes into total angular momentum representations as 
\begin{align}\begin{split}
{\rm ker}_{{R_A}}[\bar\sigma^\mu D_\mu]=\bigoplus_{\mu\in \Delta^+_{R_A}[T_3,I_3]}\left[\langle \mu,T_3\rangle-\frac{1}{2}\right]~,
\end{split}\end{align}
where $[j]$ is the spin-$j$ representation. Summing over the irreducible components $R_A$, we find 
\begin{align}\begin{split}
{\rm ker}_R[\bar\sigma^\mu D_\mu]&=\bigoplus_A\bigoplus_{\mu\in \Delta^+_{R_A}[T_3,I_3]}\left[\langle \mu,T_3\rangle-\frac{1}{2}\right]=\bigoplus_{\mu \in \Delta^+_R[T_3,I_3]}\left[\langle \mu,T_3\rangle-\frac{1}{2}\right]^{\oplus n_R(\mu)}~.
\end{split}\end{align}
\qed
